ef 17592186241003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133874 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133875 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1045 "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134005 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3a450e6c-560e-5c98-a021-778f5de2c088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7a8c44b-b300-5e66-9428-e03f400ebf5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134064 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1061 32457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91e83dab-899c-5474-8059-ebdcc7a5278a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134818 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134818 #edb/ref 1061 32458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134818 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4240c631-050e-58b5-968f-46d616a5f794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134818 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134819 #edb/ref 1061 32459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e60ded4d-fa5d-5393-8f16-7c1cc6abe2dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134820 #edb/ref 1061 32460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d1a7540-bb8b-5865-a0e0-852beed15e26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134821 #edb/ref 1061 32485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20a627a4-468b-5c73-a4aa-e3ab9c87207e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1061 33420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97501e93-7a73-5f78-8d81-1f3d7a340746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134885 #edb/ref 1061 37245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b89a94ac-0dd4-505c-b266-41783d527047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135105 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $V$ be an inner product space with the inner product $\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\rangle.$ If \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n is a set of mutually orthogonal vectors in $V,$  then the **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto the subspace \n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nwith respect to the inner product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{1}⟩}{⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{1}⟩}{a}_{1}+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{2}⟩}{⟨{a}_{2},{a}_{2}⟩}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{n}⟩}{⟨{a}_{n},{a}_{n}⟩}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_i\\}$ for $i=1, 2, \\cdots, n.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- This is entirely analogous to the corresponding formula for Euclidean spaces.\n\n- As in Euclidean spaces, the formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal with respect to the inner product!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135105 #edb/ref 1065 5162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135105 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9b462101-527b-5e26-bcd4-89b8459552bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135105 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135106 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $V$ is a vector space equipped with an inner product $\\left\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\right\\rangle$ and that $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in V.$ The **distance $d$ between $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ with respect to the inner product** is defined by the *norm* of $\\mathbf{v}- \\mathbf{w}.$ That is,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖v-w‖=\\sqrt{⟨v-w,v-w⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $V = \\mathcal{C}[0,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_0^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe distance between the functions $f(t)=4t$ and $g(t)=t+1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖4t-(t+1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t-1,3t-1⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (3t-1)^{3}|}_{0}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot [(8)-(-1)]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, given an orthogonal set \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  V\n$$\n\nand a vector $\\mathbf x \\in V,$  the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace** \n\n$$\nS=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{black} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135106 #edb/ref 1065 5163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135106 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ca26c36-a2e4-51d5-ae7d-a4b59234cedb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135106 #edb/ref 1067 "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135107 #edb/ref 1064 "The orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ (or simply onto a vector $\\mathbf{a}$) is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the distance defined by the inner product.\n\nAlthough we've considered abstract vector spaces with any possible inner product in this lesson, we can still visualize this situation with the following diagram:\n\n![](images/09/099a0bb20552824d2376994c3eb7145e48bbf78552d16de39fec44bda6b766f2.png)\n\nAs shown in the diagram, the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nFurthermore, $\\| \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ will be the smallest possible distance (with respect to our inner product) from $\\mathbf{x}$ to any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135107 #edb/ref 1065 5343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135107 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "629bc20f-76c3-5217-b763-e4ab0c831a8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135107 #edb/ref 1067 "The Intuition Behind Orthogonal Projections in Inner Product Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135141 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the functions \n\n$$\nf_1(t)=t,\\qquad f_2(t)=t^2+1, \\qquad h(t)= 5t^3,\n$$\n\nin the vector space $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1],$ where the inner product is defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^1 x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's find the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto the subspace\n\n$$\nS= \\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\},\n$$\n\n \n\ngiven that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to this inner product. \n\nSince the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,h(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,h(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,h(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute these projections:\n\n- First, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot t \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote: We used the fact that the integrand in the numerator is an odd function, so its integral over $[-1,1]$ must be zero.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,h(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135141 #edb/ref 1065 6130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135141 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "975c7894-a67f-5528-875e-2e55a94d99b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135141 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238150 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09b29cd5-55b5-5722-ba90-23f769345723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238169 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238169 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238169 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ccb496a-1f3e-5f9c-9be5-7756bb97a385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238193 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238193 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238193 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10e4eb1a-f07b-5500-9b6b-bb104419f1ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238235 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238235 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238235 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1597005a-083a-5b2f-904c-d3601ce7fd24" #edb/ref 17592186045